menu "Transforms (encodings, formats)"

#
# Transforms are the encodings and file formats around the algorithms: they
# turn material produced elsewhere into something indexable. Nothing here
# derives, protects or verifies anything.
#

config CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_KEYLOG
	bool "SSLKEYLOGFILE key log (draft-ietf-tls-keylogfile-05)"
	default y
	help
	  Parse the SSLKEYLOGFILE format into one global, read-only hash table
	  keyed by the 32-byte ClientHello Random — the cnonce a TLS decoder
	  already has once it has parsed a ClientHello — and look secrets up by
	  (cnonce, label). Covers the TLS 1.3 traffic, handshake, early and
	  exporter secrets, the CLIENT_RANDOM master secret and
	  PMS_CLIENT_RANDOM pre-master secret of TLS 1.2 and below, and the
	  ECH_SECRET / ECH_CONFIG labels of the draft's ECH section.

	  An entry holds the two key schedules as a union discriminated by the
	  version its labels imply, with every buffer cut to the ceiling the
	  RFCs set for it (modules/net/tls/bounds.h), so a secret costs no
	  allocation and an entry is a fixed ~1.6 KB.

	  This is how a passive dissector decrypts a capture it has the secrets
	  for: the protocol module asks this table instead of deriving from a
	  private key it does not have. Loading is a one-time startup step
	  (keylog_init() reads $SSLKEYLOGFILE); lookups afterwards take no lock.

	  The table holds plaintext session secrets in this process's heap for
	  as long as it runs, so anything that can read the process can read
	  every session it decoded. Enable it for analysis builds; leave it off
	  where that is not what you want to be true.

	  If unsure, say N.

config CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_KEYLOG_BITS
	int "Key log hash table order (buckets = 2^n)"
	range 4 20
	default 12
	depends on CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_KEYLOG
	help
	  Number of buckets in the key log table, as a power of two. The table
	  is a bare array of queue heads, so it costs one pointer per bucket:
	  the default 12 is 4096 buckets, 32 KB of bss, and keeps chains at one
	  or two entries for the few thousand connections a capture's key log
	  usually describes.

	  Raise it for a log covering far more connections than that; there is
	  no rehashing, so the chains simply grow.

config CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_HPACK
	bool "HPACK header compression (RFC 7541), decoding side"
	default n
	help
	  HPACK: the header compression HTTP/2 carries its metadata under.
	  Turns the opaque bytes of a header block back into a name and a value
	  at a time — the integer and string codings of §5, the Huffman code of
	  Appendix B, the static table of Appendix A, and the dynamic table the
	  two endpoints build together as they go.

	  Decoding only. Nothing in this tree sends HTTP/2, and an encoder
	  would be a second table and a second set of bugs for a message no
	  process here writes.

	  The tables are generated straight out of the RFC and checked in, so
	  building needs no python and no network; the generator is in the
	  module directory, and it verifies the automaton it emits against
	  every Huffman-coded string in Appendix C before writing anything.

	  Nothing here allocates. A dynamic table's memory is the caller's and
	  its size is fixed when the table is made, so an encoder can neither
	  choose how much this process holds nor how long it holds it — which
	  matters, because everything HPACK asks a decoder to keep is chosen by
	  the peer.

	  This is what modules/net/http2 decodes HEADERS with. Enable it with
	  that; on its own it is a library nothing calls.

	  If unsure, say N.

config CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_HPACK_FIELD_MAX
	int "(HPACK) largest header name or value reconstructed, bytes"
	default 65535
	range 256 65535
	depends on CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_HPACK
	help
	  The ceiling on one name and on one value, separately. It has to be
	  the decoder's own number: a Huffman-coded string says how many bytes
	  it occupies on the wire and not how many it expands to, so there is
	  no bound available from the field itself before it is decoded.

	  The default is the largest a length can be and still fit the two u16
	  fields a dynamic table entry keeps, which is why the range stops
	  there. It is deliberately generous rather than tuned: a field above
	  it is refused, and refusing one field breaks that direction's dynamic
	  table for good — every block after it is decoded against a table the
	  encoder and this decoder no longer share — so the cost of setting it
	  too low is losing every header of a connection rather than losing one.

	  Real traffic reaches further into this than a first guess suggests.
	  The HTTP/2 corpus in this tree (tools/testing/tls/un/test-h2-*) sends
	  a header block of 22 KB across a HEADERS and a CONTINUATION, and an
	  earlier default of 8192 refused it — which is the shape to expect:
	  not a header that is a little too long, but one connection reporting
	  no headers at all from a stream onwards.

	  It sizes the per-worker staging (struct hpack_scratch), which is four
	  times this many bytes — 256 KB at the default — and exists once per
	  thread of control rather than once per connection. Lower it for a
	  deployment running many workers on a small machine, knowing what the
	  paragraph above says the cost is.

config CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_QPACK
	bool "QPACK field compression (RFC 9204), decoding side"
	default n
	help
	  QPACK: the field compression HTTP/3 carries its metadata under.
	  Turns the opaque bytes of an encoded field section back into a name
	  and a value at a time — the integer and string codings of §4.1, the
	  static table of Appendix A, and the dynamic table the encoder builds
	  over its own unidirectional stream.

	  Decoding only, for HPACK's reason: nothing in this tree sends
	  HTTP/3. What QPACK's split of reading from writing buys a passive
	  decoder is that an encoded field section never modifies the table,
	  so one that references insertions still in flight is simply held
	  and offered again — only the encoder stream can poison the state.

	  The static table is generated straight out of RFC 9204 and checked
	  in; the Huffman automaton is HPACK's (RFC 9204 §4.1.2 adopts
	  RFC 7541 Appendix B unchanged), included from its directory so
	  there is one generator and one table to trust.

	  Nothing here allocates. A dynamic table's memory is the caller's
	  and its size is fixed when the table is made, so an encoder can
	  neither choose how much this process holds nor how long.

	  This is what modules/net/http3 decodes HEADERS with. Enable it with
	  that; on its own it is a library nothing calls.

	  If unsure, say N.

config CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_QPACK_FIELD_MAX
	int "(QPACK) largest field name or value reconstructed, bytes"
	default 65535
	range 256 65535
	depends on CRYPTO_TRANSFORM_QPACK
	help
	  The ceiling on one name and on one value, separately, for the same
	  reason HPACK's exists: a Huffman-coded string says how many bytes it
	  occupies on the wire and not how many it expands to.

	  The consequences of a refusal are gentler here than on HPACK: a
	  field section that will not fit loses that section and nothing
	  else, because sections never write the dynamic table. A field
	  arriving on the *encoder stream* past this ceiling still breaks the
	  direction for good, exactly as an HPACK refusal does, which is why
	  the default is the largest the table entry's u16 lengths can hold
	  rather than something tuned.

	  It sizes the per-worker staging (struct qpack_scratch), four times
	  this many bytes, once per thread of control.

endmenu
